f powers of consecutive primes, including both the same or different values of $k$. We give a simple heuristic model for estimating the density of multiply-gleeful numbers, we present empirical data in support of our heuristics, and offer some new conjectures.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen capped swaps lock in · Evidence Press (https://www.evidencepress.org/releases/pass-and-swap-bounded-swaps/)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Bounded swaps in pass-and-swap queues: counterexamples to a conjecture of Dorsman and Gardner, and a proof for bipartite swapping graphs with odd caps\n\nCapping the swaps in pass-and-swap queues can trap the system in arrangements the product-form theory excludes: a 2024 conjecture fails for every cap of two or more, exactly on rings of 2kw+1 jobs and beyond, but holds for bipartite swapping graphs with odd caps. ... Two odd cycles $C_{2a+1}$, $C_{2b+1}$ joined by a path of length $L$ violate the conjecture at $w=a+b+L+1$ (Theorem 6.1)  | proof\n\nPress release · 20 October 2026 · version 1.0.0-candidate\n\n# Bounded swaps in pass-and-swap queues: counterexamples to a conjecture of Dorsman and Gardner, and a proof for bipartite swapping graphs with odd caps\n\nCapping the swaps in pass-and-swap queues can trap the system in arrangements the product-form theory excludes: a 2024 conjecture fails for every cap of two or more, exactly on rings of 2kw+1 jobs and beyond, but holds for bipartite swapping graphs with odd caps.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis page explains, in order: what pass-and-swap queues are for, what Dorsman and Gardner conjectured about them, what this release found (counterexamples, an exact rule for rings, and a positive theorem), and what has and has not been checked.\n\nWhat these queues model. Many service systems have to match jobs with resources that can handle them: computing tasks with servers that hold the right data, patients with compatible donors, riders with drivers. Queueing theory tries to predict how such systems behave in the long run: how long queues get, and how often each kind of job is being served. Rings are not the only problem: two odd rings joined by a path also fail, and a complete computer census of all connected networks with up to seven jobs finds 1,355 failing combinations of network and cap.\n\nWhere the conjecture does hold. None of the failures has a bipartite swapping graph, one whose jobs split into two groups with swaps allowed only across groups. The release proves that the conjecture is true for bipartite swapping graphs when the cap is odd (including the simplest cap, $w=1$) and every position is served. Exact computation on every bipartite network with up to nine jobs finds no exception for even caps either, so that case is stated as a new conjecture.\n\nWhat happens inside the trap. When all jobs are served at the same rate, the long-run behaviour inside the trap still has an explicit simple formula. When service rates vary from job to job within a queue, the natural repair, rescaling the original product-form weights by one constant for each arrangement, provably does not work, although on the smallest example the error is small. \"(PR)\" below means every position of every queue is served at a positive rate.\n\nResult  | Status\n--- | ---\nFor every $w\\ge2$ the balanced states of $C_{2w+1}$ form a closed class of height $w+1$, for every admissible rate function; under (PR) it is a single recurrent class with $(2w+2)!/(w!)^2$ states. Conjecture 1 is false for every $w\\ge2$ (Theorem 4.1, Corollary 4.2)  | proof\nCycles under (PR): $C_n$ has a recurrent class of height greater than $w$ if and only if $n\\equiv 1 \\pmod{2w}$, and it is then unique. For $w\\ge2$ (where every cycle satisfies the colouring hypothesis) the conjecture fails on $C_n$ exactly when $n\\equiv1\\pmod{2w}$; $C_9$ at $w=2$ refutes the repair \"odd girth larger than $2w+1$\" (Theorem 5.2)  | proof\nTwo odd cycles $C_{2a+1}$, $C_{2b+1}$ joined by a path of length $L$ violate the conjecture at $w=a+b+L+1$ (Theorem 6.1)  | proof\nAll connected graphs with at most 7 vertices: 1,355 violating (graph, cap) pairs, none bipartite, none at $w=1$ (Table 3)  | exact census\nBipartite $G$, odd $w$, (PR): every state of height greater than $w$ is transient (Theorem 7.1).     python3 -S -O -B negative_controls.py      # the checks reject a wrong cap and a wrong cycle rule`\n\n`verify_all.py` copies the verifiers to a temporary directory, reruns each one and compares its certificate byte for byte with the shipped copy. To understand the mathematics, start with Section 1.4 (the five-cycle example) and Lemma 3.3 of the paper; `AI_INDEX.md` maps each result to its evidence.\n\n## The most valuable next projects\n\n  1. Prove or refute Conjecture 7.2: bipartite swapping graphs with even caps.\n  2. Characterise all pairs $(G,w)$ with a recurrent class of excessive height; the cycle criterion and the two-cycle family suggest a rule in terms of closed walks and $w$.\n  3. Decide the conjecture when only the heads of the queues are served, without the positive-rates assumption.\n  4. Find the stationary law on the counterexample class under heterogeneous rates, and extend the analysis to the random, state-dependent caps that model random order of service.\n\n## What is in the evidence package\n\nThe manuscript (PDF and LaTeX source, with TikZ figures), fifteen exact verifiers and their JSON certificates, the one-command replay script, semantic negative controls, a novelty statement and source map, a rating-free summary of the internal review, claim, status, assurance, provenance and replay records, an agent index, licences (CC0-1.0 for prose, manuscript and data; MIT for code) and SHA-256 sums. The same files are in the GitHub release and the Zenodo record.\n\n## Verification status\n\nUnrefereed AI-assisted mathematical candidate. Written proofs and exact certificates have the stated scope; internal AI-assisted and adversarial review only. External specialist review, independent reproduction and formal verification have not been established.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOnline Number Theory Seminar (https://ntrg.math.unideb.hu/Luca2026abstract.pdf)\nciteturn6search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Luca: Multiply Gleeful numbers. ... A number is called k-gleeful if fk(n) > 0 and multiply gleeful if ... version of a conjecture of Moore and Sorenson from the preprint arXiv:2507.09012v1, July 2025.\nOnline Number Theory Seminar\n23 January 2026. – 17:00-17:50\nF. Luca: Multiply Gleeful numbers.\nFor positive integers k and n let fk(n) be the number of ways of representing n as a sum of k\npowers of consecutive primes. A number is called k-gleeful if fk(n) > 0 and multiply gleeful if\nfk(n) > 1 or fk(n)fk′(n) > 0 for some positive integers k < k′. Under Schinzel’s hypothesis H, we\nshow that there are infinitely many positive integers n such that f2(n)f4(n) > 0. Under the same\nassumption we show that lim supn→∞f2(n) = ∞. This gives a conditional proof of a stronger\nversion of a conjecture of Moore and Sorenson from the preprint arXiv:2507.09012v1, July 2025.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe number of sum-free subsets of lattice cubes (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23544)\nciteturn6academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Date: Mon Aug 24 17:46:57 2026 ... This verifies a conjecture of Elsholtz and Rackham. Our proof combines the dual weights constructed by Keevash and Lim in their work for $M([n]^d)$, a one-dimensional counting estimate due to Ghosal, a bipartite swapping lemma of Zhao, and a strong fractional entropy inequality of Madiman and Tetali, and it avoids the use of the container lemma or deriving a stability theorem first.\nTitle: The number of sum-free subsets of lattice cubes\nAuthors: Haoran Luo\nDate: Mon Aug 24 17:46:57 2026\n\nA subset of the $d$-dimensional lattice cube $[n]^d$ is sum-free if it contains no solution to the equation $x+y=z$. We study the total number of such subsets. For $d=1$, Cameron and Erdős conjectured that the number of sum-free subsets of $[n]$ is $O(2^{n/2})$, and this was proved independently by Green and Sapozhenko. A recent work by Ghosal solved the case $d = 2$. In this paper, we consider all remaining dimensions and prove that for every fixed integer $d \\geqslant 3$, the number of sum-free subsets of $[n]^d$ is $2^{M([n]^d) + O_d(n^{d-1})}$, where $M([n]^d)$ is the maximum possible size of a sum-free subset of $[n]^d$. This verifies a conjecture of Elsholtz and Rackham. Our proof combines the dual weights constructed by Keevash and Lim in their work for $M([n]^d)$, a one-dimensional counting estimate due to Ghosal, a bipartite swapping lemma of Zhao, and a strong fractional entropy inequality of Madiman and Tetali, and it avoids the use of the container lemma or deriving a stability theorem first.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmath/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex at main · openai/math · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 3 days ago; \\begin{proof}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n\nThe degree bound follows from that of $\\varphi_{u,y}$. At zero,\n\n\\[\n\n\\vec{P}_u(0)=\\delta_u(I-M_0)+\\delta_uM_0=\\delta_u.\n\n\\]\n\nApplying \\eqref{eq:swaps-row} to $\\zeta$ gives\n\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:swaps-form-decomposition}\n\n\\langle \\vec{P}_u(T), \\zeta\\rangle=((I-M_0)\\zeta)_u+\n\n\\sum_{\\substack{y\\in\\mathcal B\\\\M_0[u,y]\\ne 0}}\n\nM_0[u,y]\\varphi_{u,y}(T)\\zeta_y.\n\n\\end{equation}\n\nAt $T=t$, all summands with $a_y(t)\\ne d_u(t)$ vanish. If\n\n$\\widetilde\\xi$ is supported on $S^{\\mathcal B}_{t,g}$ with\n\n$g\\ne d_u(t)$, each remaining summand is zero on $\\widetilde\\xi$.\n\nThe hypothesis $C^{\\mathcal B}_{t,g}\\xi=\\vec{0}$ also gives\n\n$C^{\\mathcal B}\\widetilde\\xi=\\vec{0}$, so\n\n$(I-M_0)\\widetilde\\xi=\\vec{0}$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:swaps-matrix}.\n\nThis proves the implication.\n\nFor the final assertion, two vectors with the same measurements under\n\n$C^{\\mathcal B}$ differ by an element of its kernel. Thus\n\n$(I-M_0)\\zeta$ is determined by $C^{\\mathcal B}\\zeta$. The remaining\n\nterms in \\eqref{eq:swaps-form-decomposition} at $T=t$ use only\n\n$\\zeta|_{S^{\\mathcal B}_{t,d_u(t)}}$.\n\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\subsection{Choosing the variable and factor groups}\\label{subsec:choosing-labels}\n\nWe now choose the families $(a_v)_{v\\in[n]}$ and\n\n$(d_f)_{f\\in\\mathcal F}$ from\n\nSection~\\ref{subsec:incidence-layout}, and set\n\n\\[\n\nu_{f,v}=(a_v,d_f)\\qquad((f,v)\\in\\mathcal A).\n\n\\]\n\nThese choices have two requirements. First, if a label occurs $c_u$\n\ntimes, its incidences need $c_u$ different positions in copies of the\n\nauxiliary array. Thus the number of copies needed is\n\n\\[\n\nc_u=\\#\\{(f,v)\\in\\mathcal A:u_{f,v}=u\\},\\qquad\n\nh=\\max_{u\\in\\mathcal B}c_u.\n\n\\]\n\nSecond, the actual variable group $\\{v:a_v(t)=g\\}$ must be small.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJoel Hakavuori (https://www.fulbright.fi/serve/cv-catalog-2025-26-fulbright-finland-foundation-grantees-to-us)\nciteturn6search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Professor David Speyer\n\nGRANT PERIOD:  \nSeptember 2025 – May 2026\nJoel Hakavuori\n\nPURE MATHEMATICS  \nThe Fulbright Finland Foundation and Technology Industries of Finland Centennial Foundation Graduate Fellowship\n\nSPECIALIZATION:  \nGeometry, Topology and Combinatorics\n\nFULBRIGHT PROJECT TITLE:  \nTheory and L2-Invariants\n\nFULBRIGHT PROJECT DESCRIPTION:  \nIn recent years, techniques inspired by algebraic geometry have proven effective in solving difficult enumerative problems in combinatorial geometry and topology. My doctoral research investigates how these techniques can be leveraged to study the geometric topology and L2-invariants of manifolds with non-positive curvature through the algebraic and combinatorial properties of their triangulations.\n\nKEY WORDS:  \nGeometric Topology, Combinatorics, Algebra\n\nHOME INSTITUTION AND DEPARTMENT:  \nSorbonne University, Institut de Mathématiques de Jussieu – Paris Rive Gauche (IMJ-PRG), Paris, France\n\nHOST INSTITUTION AND DEPARTMENT IN THE UNITED STATES:  \nUniversity of Michigan, Department of Mathematics, Ann Arbor, Michigan\n\nHOST/CONTACT PERSON AT THE DEPARTMENT IN THE UNITED STATES:  \nProfessor David Speyer\n\nGRANT PERIOD:  \nSeptember 2025 – May 2026\n\n>> LINK TO THE UNIVERSITY ONLINE PROFILE\n\nACADEMIC TRAINING:  \n– M.Sc., 2024, Mathematics, Aalto University, Finland  \n– B.Sc., 2023, Engineering Physics and Mathematics, Aalto University, Finland\n\nPROFESSIONAL BACKGROUND:  \n– Doctoral Candidate, Sorbonne University, Institut de Mathématiques de Jussieu – Paris Rive Gauche, Paris, France, since 2025\n\nSELECTED PUBLICATIONS:  \n– K. Adiprasito, A. Björner, J. Hakavuori, M. Margaritis, V. Welker: Subadditivity of shifts, Eilenberg-Zilber shuffle products and homology of lattices, ArXiv (2404.16643), 2024\n\nFELLOWSHIPS, HONORS, ETC.:  \n– 2025-26: Fulbright-Technology Industries of Finland Award  \n– 2025-27: Osk. Huttunen Foundation Doctoral Grant  \n– 2023: Aalto University, Combined Student Fund Scholarship  \n– 2022: Aalto University, Prof. E.J. Nyström scholarship\n\n20--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nresearchseminars.org - View talk (https://researchseminars.org/talk/CANT2026/86/)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Multiply gleeful numbers ... A number is called $k$-gleeful if $f_k(n)>0$ and multiply gleeful if $f_k(n)>1$ or $f_k(n)f_{k'}(n)>0$ for some positive integers $k< k'. ... This gives a conditional proof of a stronger version of a conjecture of Moore and Sorenson from the preprint arXiv:2507.09012v1, July 2025.\n\n# Multiply gleeful numbers\n\n### Florian Luca (Stellenbosch University, South Africa)\n\nSat Jul 18, 17:30-17:55 (3 months ago)\n\nAbstract: For positive integers $k$ and $n$ let $f_k(n)$ be the number of ways of representing $n$ as a sum of $k$ powers of consecutive primes. A number is called $k$-gleeful if $f_k(n)>0$ and multiply gleeful if $f_k(n)>1$ or $f_k(n)f_{k'}(n)>0$ for some positive integers $k< k'.$ Under Schinzel's hypothesis H, we show that there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that $f_2(n)f_4(n)>0$. Under the same assumption we show that $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} f_2(n)=\\infty$. This gives a conditional proof of a stronger version of a conjecture of Moore and Sorenson from the preprint arXiv:2507.09012v1, July 2025.\n\nnumber theory\n\nAudience: researchers in the topic\n\nCombinatorial and additive number theory seminar (CANT 2026)\n\nOrganizer:  | Mel Nathanson*\n | *contact for this listing--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn [49]: import numpy as np (https://scde.jntuh.ac.in/lmsportal/alumniuploads/assignments_29052025/NLP_Assignment.pdf)\nciteturn6search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; 'confirm': 519, 'enquiringly': 884, 'checked': 417, 'checking': 418, 'replied': 2145, 'prove': 2025, 'sending': 2270, ... y': 2567, 'backs': 211, 'continually': 548, 'gleeful': 1145, 'anticipation': 127, 'quarrel': 2048, 'favour': 986, 'co\ny': 2567, 'backs': 211, 'continually': 548, 'gleeful': 1145, 'anticipation': 127, 'quarrel': 2048, 'favour': 986, 'co\nntents': 547, 'rearranging': 2084, 'positions': 1956, 'humility': 1297, 'strides': 2507, 'rubbing': 2199, 'friend': 1\n095, 'fear': 988, 'connection': 525, 'leader': 1488, 'hats': 1219,